 ### Adaptive Sampling for Silhouettes
-A strategy using two discrete rates (base $r_0$ and max $r_1$) to improve intersection detection near silhouettes without diverging parallel execution pipelines [S10]. Sets itself apart by guaranteeing intersection detection for thin sheets via bounded gradient steps, unlike fully continuous adaptive methods. Choose when rendering isosurfaces or distance fields where silhouette accuracy is critical.
+A strategy using two discrete rates (base $r_0$ and max $r_1$) to improve intersection detection near silhouettes without diverging parallel execution pipelines [S11]. Sets itself apart by guaranteeing intersection detection for thin sheets via bounded gradient steps, unlike fully continuous adaptive methods [S11]. Choose when rendering isosurfaces or distance fields where silhouette accuracy is critical [S11].
 
 ### Adaptive Sampling with Opacity Correction
-An approach that adapts sampling frequency to the finest level cell size while keeping total samples tractable, combined with opacity correction [S16, unofficial]. Sets itself apart by being tailored for AMR (Adaptive Mesh Refinement) hierarchies, avoiding the under- or over-sampling issues of hierarchy-unaware methods. Choose when rendering AMR datasets where cell sizes vary drastically.
+An approach that adapts sampling frequency to the finest level cell size while keeping total samples tractable, combined with opacity correction [S23]. Sets itself apart by being tailored for AMR (Adaptive Mesh Refinement) hierarchies, avoiding the under- or over-sampling issues of hierarchy-unaware methods [S23]. Choose when rendering AMR datasets where cell sizes vary drastically [S23].
 
 ### Adaptive Slicing with Slant Limiting
-Calculates slant $dh = k_{max} - k_{min}$ and slices the volume into a prism, choosing sampling rates close to voxel size to improve rendering time [S17, unofficial]. Sets itself apart by using slant-limited parameters to prevent infinity at grazing angles ($k_M = 2h$), unlike applications that choose higher rates for quality. Choose when rendering forest scenes or terrain with extreme view angles.
